Network design:
The first priority was to make sure both virtual machines could communicate reliably and that the client machine used the Domain Controller as its DNS server.
I used a Host-Only or Internal Network for lab communication.
NAT was added only to the server as a second adapter when internet access was required.
The Domain Controller uses a static IP address and points to itself for DNS.
The client machine uses the Domain Controller as its only DNS server.
This setup reflects a common small enterprise Active Directory environment.

Domain Controller setup:
I created a virtual machine named DC01 and installed Windows Server with Desktop Experience.
After installation, I renamed the server to DC01 and configured a static IP address.
Once networking was confirmed, I installed the Active Directory Domain Services role and promoted the server to a Domain Controller by creating a new forest named lab.local.
After rebooting, I verified the installation by checking Active Directory Users and Computers, DNS Manager, and validating name resolution using command line tools.

Client machine setup:
I created a second virtual machine named PC01 and installed Windows 10 or Windows 11.
After renaming the computer, I configured its DNS settings to point to the Domain Controller.
I then joined the machine to the lab.local domain using domain administrator credentials.
After a successful reboot, I logged in using a domain user account.
When domain join issues occurred, I troubleshot them by checking DNS configuration, connectivity, and name resolution using ping and nslookup.

Active Directory fundamentals:
To reflect a real-world environment, I created a clear Active Directory structure.
I created organizational units for users, computers, and groups.
The client machine was moved into the Computers OU.
I created multiple test user accounts and several security groups such as Helpdesk, Finance, VPN Users, and Local Admins.
Users were added to groups based on simulated job roles.
I practiced common Service Desk tasks daily, including password resets, account unlocks, disabling and enabling accounts, and enforcing password changes at next logon.

Group Policy management:
I created and tested several Group Policy Objects to understand how policies affect users and computers.
These included a domain password and lockout policy, a desktop or wallpaper message for users, and a policy restricting access to the Control Panel for a test OU.
I validated all policies from the client machine using gpupdate and gpresult to confirm correct application.
DNS and DHCP configuration
I practiced DNS management by creating and testing custom DNS records and confirming name resolution from the client machine.
I also configured DHCP on the Domain Controller to understand IP address allocation in a domain environment.
After creating a scope and setting DNS options, I switched the client from static IP to DHCP and verified correct network configuration.

Troubleshooting and ticket simulations:
To build real employable skills, I created a ticket simulation log and intentionally broke parts of the environment.
I simulated common issues such as failed logins, locked and disabled accounts, DNS misconfiguration, time synchronization problems, disk space issues, stopped services, and Remote Desktop access problems.
Each issue was documented using a professional Service Desk ticket format that included the issue description, impact, checks performed, findings, resolution, and prevention or recommendations.
This approach helped me learn how to diagnose problems methodically and communicate solutions clearly.

PowerShell basics:
I practiced basic PowerShell commands used in IT Support environments.
This included listing Active Directory users, unlocking accounts, resetting passwords in a test environment, and reviewing event logs.
The focus was on understanding what each command does and using PowerShell safely and confidently.

GitHub documentation:
I published this project on GitHub to demonstrate practical experience and professional documentation.
The repository includes written documentation, clean and anonymized screenshots, and troubleshooting ticket simulations.
No sensitive information such as real passwords, product keys, or personal data was uploaded.
The repository structure reflects a real technical project and shows how I plan, build, configure, and troubleshoot a Windows domain environment.

